window.WG_DOOLITTLE_SRC="data:image/webp;base64,";
window.WL_SOURCES_URL=URL.createObjectURL(new Blob(["<!doctype html><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><title>Witness Lexington sources</title><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\"># Lexington historical passages for WL-D1\n\nPublic sources below were read on October 9, 2026, after read-only network access was enabled. Each entry gives the passage location, what it supports, and its limits. The product spec and private BrainLift are not historical evidence. Ellipses mark trims in primary-source quotations. A passage summary is a paraphrase, not historical dialogue.\n\n## Night alarm and escape\n\n`[NIGHT]`: Lexington History Museums, [*Paul Revere's Ride*, April 18th, 1775 section](https://www.lexingtonhistory.org/paulrevere). The paragraphs beginning \"British soldiers in Boston\", \"Revere arrived in Lexington\", and \"Revere made his way back\" describe the warning, Adams and Hancock lodging at Clarke's parsonage, and their departure to safety. The last passage includes:\n\n&gt; …Revere personally escorted the men to a nearby safe house shortly before dawn…\n\nThe museum identifies later recollections among its sources. Its arrival time differs from the NPS timeline's 12:30 a.m. entry, so the game uses the night of April 18 to 19 without an exact arrival time. Eli's route, horse, errands, encounters, and departure staging remain visibly dramatized. The game does not quote a reconstructed parsonage conversation as primary testimony.\n\n## Lexington setting and numbers\n\n`[SETTING]` and `[NUMBERS]` use these passages, with their different scopes retained:\n\n| Source and passage locator | Relevant passage, paraphrased | What the student can conclude |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Thomas Rice Willard, deposition dated April 23, 1775, in Abraham Tomlinson, *The Military Journals of Two Private Soldiers*, 1855, supplement \"Official Papers Concerning the Skirmishes at Lexington and Concord\", printed pages 97 to 98. [Public transcription](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/20636/20636-h/20636-h.htm#page097). | From Harrington's window before sunrise, Willard estimated about 400 regulars in one body approaching about 100 militia. | Contemporary estimates, explicitly qualified by the observer. This supports approximately 400 approaching regulars, not an exact roll. |\n| John Robins, deposition dated April 24, 1775, same Tomlinson supplement, printed pages 100 to 101. [Public transcription](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/20636/20636-h/20636-h.htm#page100). | In Parker's front rank, Robins estimated about 1,000 approaching regulars, expressly qualifying it as what he thought. | A much larger eyewitness estimate. It is evidence of count disagreement, not a verified strength. |\n| NPS, [*April 19, 1775*, 4:30 a.m. and 5:00 a.m. entries](https://www.nps.gov/mima/learn/historyculture/april-19-1775.htm). | Smith detached around 200 light infantry ahead; regulars approaching Lexington encountered 77 militia on the Green. Pitcairn sent some companies toward them while others continued along the road. | A smaller detached-force estimate and a specific militia count. Do not equate every soldier on the road with the companies confronting the militia. |\n| NPS, [*Embattled Farmers*, opening historical overview](https://www.nps.gov/articles/embattled-farmers-and-the-shot-heard-round-the-world-the-battles-of-lexington-and-concord-teaching-with-historic-places.htm). | Gage ordered 700 soldiers on the expedition to Concord. | This is the whole expedition, not the advance force on the Green. |\n| Edward Thoroton Gould, deposition dated April 25, 1775, same Tomlinson supplement, printed pages 114 to 115. [Public transcription](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/20636/20636-h/20636-h.htm#page114). | Gould estimated 60 or 70 armed provincials at Lexington; he could not say exactly which party fired first. | A British officer's smaller militia estimate, not a settled count or verdict. |\n| Paul Revere, deposition of 1775, MHS item 98, page 2. [Chosen transcription](https://www.masshist.org/database/viewer.php?item_id=98&amp;mode=transcript#page2). | Revere estimated about 50 or 60 militia as he passed through them. | Another observer's approximate count. |\n\nBarker's diary excerpt in NPS Reading 2, cited under `[BARKER]` below, puts the people formed on the Common at about 200 to 300 (printed as \"2 and 300\"). That is substantially higher than Gould's, Revere's, and Willard's militia estimates. Attribute it to Barker rather than silently replace it with 80.\n\nWillard's chosen transcription reads:\n\n&gt; …saw (as I suppose) about four hundred regulars in one body…\n\nThe cited eyewitness estimates of approaching regulars span about 400 to 1,000 (Willard and Robins); NPS describes about 200 in the detached advance force and 700 in the whole expedition. The cited militia estimates span about 50 to 300, with Barker at the high end; NPS gives 77. Force scopes and observations differ. These are reported estimates, not verified minimum and maximum rosters. The opening says \"Hundreds of regulars approach the militia. Estimates vary.\" The numbers card retains the inherited approximate 400-versus-80 reconstruction and displays this source note, including these estimates, ranges, and references, before scoring. It does not treat the reconstruction as an exact headcount. Preserve the approved scoring cells and thresholds.\n\n## Formation\n\n`[FORMATION]`: [*Battles of Lexington and Concord*, Lexington section](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battles_of_Lexington_and_Concord#Lexington), paragraph beginning \"Parker positioned his company\". Its passage describes parade-ground formation in plain sight, rather than concealment behind walls, and cites John R. Galvin, *The Minute Men*, pages 120 to 124. This is a checked public secondary passage; Galvin's book was not independently consulted.\n\nA supporting primary account is the April 25, 1775 joint deposition of Nathaniel Parkhurst, Jonas Parker, John Munroe Jr., and eleven other named deponents, in the [Tomlinson supplement, printed pages 103 to 104](https://www.gutenberg.org/files/20636/20636-h/20636-h.htm#page103). It describes the company forming on the parade facing the approaching regulars, then dispersing. It is a different document from John Parker's deposition below. Exact model spacing remains a reconstruction.\n\n## Commands and named-person movements\n\n`[COMMAND]`: [the public Lexington section](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battles_of_Lexington_and_Concord#Lexington), paragraph beginning \"British Marine Lieutenant Jesse Adair\". It describes Adair leading the advance companies onto the Common and states:\n\n&gt; Major Pitcairn then arrived from the rear of the advance force.\n\nThat paragraph cites David Hackett Fischer, *Paul Revere's Ride*, 1994, pages 189 to 190. The next paragraph describes shouting, Parker's raspy command, and some men not hearing it. It cites Sanderson and Willard depositions and the *Journals of the Continental Congress*, May 11, 1775. This packet cites the accessible secondary passage for those details, not an independently checked Fischer page or a primary medical diagnosis. Dialogue and performed voice volume remain dramatized. Willard's primary account above also says he did not understand an officer's words; this supports the hearing task without identifying that officer as Parker.\n\n## Outcome\n\n`[OUTCOME]`: NPS, [*April 19, 1775*, 5:00 a.m. entry](https://www.nps.gov/mima/learn/historyculture/april-19-1775.htm), describes eight militia killed and ten wounded after volleys and a bayonet rush. [*Battles of Lexington and Concord*, British advance to Concord section](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battles_of_Lexington_and_Concord#British_advance_to_Concord), first paragraph, supplies the single British soldier wounded and cites Benjamin Quarles, *The Negro in the American Revolution*, page 10. This is a checked public secondary passage; Quarles's book was not independently consulted. These figures concern Lexington, not all fighting on April 19.\n\n## Officers' control and Barker's account\n\n`[CONTROL]` and `[BARKER]`: NPS, [*Embattled Farmers*, Reading 2: More Than One Side to Every Story](https://www.nps.gov/articles/embattled-farmers-and-the-shot-heard-round-the-world-the-battles-of-lexington-and-concord-teaching-with-historic-places.htm), Lt. John Barker's diary excerpt, April 19 entry. NPS cites \"Diary of a British Soldier\", *Atlantic Monthly*, April 1877, volume 39. The relevant trimmed passage is:\n\n&gt; …they fired one or two shots, upon which our Men without any orders rushed in upon them…\n\nBarker attributes the first shots to the militia and describes the subsequent rush as occurring without orders. This is his testimony, not a settled first-shot finding. Use this NPS excerpt for Barker rather than the Wikipedia summary's narrower shot count. The [public British advance to Concord section](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battles_of_Lexington_and_Concord#British_advance_to_Concord), paragraph beginning \"Pitcairn's companies\", describes officers losing control and later restoring order, citing Fischer, pages 198 to 200. That checked secondary passage supports the control fact card. No private motive or officer's first-shot order is asserted.\n\n## The specific militia deposition\n\n`[MILITIA]`: John Parker, *Deposition of Captain John Parker Concerning the Battle at Lexington*, April 25, 1775, document No. 4. [DocsTeach transcript and image](https://docsteach.org/document/john-parker-lexington/); [National Archives identifier 595246](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/595246). Collection: Massachusetts State Papers, Papers of the Continental Congress, 1774 to 1789, Record Group 360, National Archives, Washington, DC. The dated transcript, document number, and full citation were checked on DocsTeach. The National Archives catalog link is its supplied record locator.\n\nParker says he ordered dispersal and no firing, then attributes an unprovoked attack to the regulars. The chosen transcript includes:\n\n&gt; …I immediately ordered our Militia to disperse and not to fire…\n\n&gt; …without receiving any Provocation therefor from us…\n\nLabel the inherited `depositions` card \"John Parker's sworn deposition, April 25, 1775; summary\". Preserve its scoring ID. Its summary supports the militia account while the opposing Barker account remains available. This is Parker's claim, not a settled verdict. Do not substitute the separate joint deposition or imply Parker witnessed every possible shooter.\n\n## Revere transcription choices\n\n`[R75]`: [MHS item 98, transcript](https://www.masshist.org/database/viewer.php?item_id=98&amp;mode=transcript#page3), manuscript page 3. The displayed transcription was retrieved directly from MHS. It describes hearing a report, turning, seeing smoke in front of the troops, and being unable to see the militia because of a house. A trimmed example is:\n\n&gt; …one gun was fired, I heard the report, turned my head…\n\nThe house statement is part of a longer sentence, so do not present the seed's house fragment as a complete sentence without ellipses.\n\n`[R98]`: Paul Revere's letter to Jeremy Belknap, 1798, manuscript held by MHS as item 99. Choose the [MASSAR transcription](https://massar.org/a-letter-from-col-paul-revere-to-the-corresponding-secretary-jeremy-belknap-on-the-battle-of-lexington/), paragraph beginning \"In their Front\", consistently retaining `&amp;`. Its text includes:\n\n&gt; …I saw, &amp; heard, a Gun fired, which appeared to be a Pistol.\n\nThe MASSAR page visually marks the whole paragraph as struck through. Retain that editorial flag when displaying its excerpt. The [MHS manuscript transcription](https://www.masshist.org/database/viewer.php?item_id=99&amp;mode=transcript#page6) was also retrieved and includes the passage, but is not the chosen quotation transcription for the 1798 card. Both accounts come from Revere; the later letter is not independent confirmation. Neither establishes who pulled a trigger.\n\n## Source and review limits\n\nThe seven challenged IDs now have public historical references and relevant passages in this design packet. The count gap is resolved with Willard's qualified estimate and the differing NPS scope, not by treating the spec as evidence. Source completion belongs to this design review and is not deferred to a playable slice.\n\nWikipedia is used as an explicitly identified secondary account, with its book citations recorded as citations rather than falsely claimed readbacks. Primary transcriptions are attributed to MHS, MASSAR, DocsTeach, NPS's diary excerpt, and the specified 1855 Tomlinson edition. No source was uploaded or edited. Some direct HTTP fetches returned 403 or 502; the browser research tool supplied the public DocsTeach, MASSAR, and Wikipedia passages. This packet does not claim every referenced book or catalog landing page was independently opened.\n\n## Doolittle image\n\n`[DOOLITTLE]`: Amos Doolittle, *The Battle of Lexington, April 19, 1775. Plate I*, Yale University Art Gallery object 2482, accession 1920.25a-d. The inherited local image, object link, IIIF provenance, and CC0 rights statement are registered in [CREDITS.md](CREDITS.md#amos-doolittle-the-battle-of-lexington-april-19-1775-plate-i-1775). The picture shows how an artist pictured the British firing. It cannot prove who fired first. No new network readback was made in WL-S3.\n\n# Credits &amp; licenses — Witness Lexington\n\n## Amos Doolittle, *The Battle of Lexington, April 19, 1775. Plate I* (1775)\n\n- Artist / engraver: Amos Doolittle (American, 1754–1832).\n- Source institution: Yale University Art Gallery, accession 1920.25a–d (Plate I is canvas 1 of that set). Object record: https://artgallery.yale.edu/collections/objects/2482\n- Image fetched at build time from Yale's IIIF: `https://images.collections.yale.edu/iiif/2/yuag:8f8444c1-483c-4a4a-b05e-b1cf2f03eb39/full/,1100/0/default.jpg` (manifest: https://manifests.collections.yale.edu/yuag/obj/2482).\n- Rights (per Yale IIIF manifest): Public Domain; Creative Commons Zero (CC0 1.0) — `https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/`. Yale also notes: \"Data Provided about Yale University Art Gallery collections are public domain. Rights restrictions may apply to cultural works or images of those works.\" (the work itself is 1775 U.S. public domain).\n- Local files: `assets/doolittle-plate1.webp` (1000 px wide, used by the game and inlined in the bundle), `assets/doolittle-plate1.jpg` (1406 px original download).\n\n## Historical source register for WL-D1\n\nSource IDs below cover every historical card, proposed caption, document label, and named-character context in `GAME_DESIGN.md`. Fictional observations and dialogue are explicitly tagged there. [SOURCES.md](SOURCES.md) supplies accessible historical references, passage locators, excerpts or paraphrases, and limitations. Public source texts were checked on October 9, 2026. Neither the product spec nor the private BrainLift serves as historical evidence.\n\n| ID | Document or source | Covered labels and claims | Text policy and verification status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `[R75]` | Paul Revere, deposition, 1775. Massachusetts Historical Society [item 98](https://www.masshist.org/database/98); [chosen transcript and passage](SOURCES.md#revere-transcription-choices). | `revere1775`; MHS item 98 label; smoke in front of troops and the house obstructing Revere's militia view | MHS transcription retrieved and checked. Use full sentences or marked trims; the packet gives a trimmed example. Design narrative uses paraphrases. |\n| `[R98]` | Paul Revere, letter to Jeremy Belknap, 1798, manuscript held by MHS as [item 99](https://www.masshist.org/database/99). [Chosen MASSAR transcription and passage](SOURCES.md#revere-transcription-choices). | `revere1798`; MHS manuscript item 99 label; apparent pistol and subsequent firing; later account by the same witness | Choose MASSAR wording with `&amp;` consistently. Public transcript checked; preserve its struck-through paragraph flag. MHS identifies the manuscript, not the chosen quotation transcription. This is not independent confirmation of `[R75]`. |\n| `[BARKER]` | Lt. John Barker's diary, April 19 entry, in NPS, *Embattled Farmers*, Reading 2; NPS cites *Atlantic Monthly*, April 1877, volume 39. [Checked excerpt and citation](SOURCES.md#officers-control-and-barkers-account). | `barker`; diary label; attribution of first firing to the militia | Barker says the militia fired **one or two shots** first. No narrower count. Selected NPS transcription is quoted with marked trims. This is his claim, not a settled verdict. |\n| `[MILITIA]` | John Parker, *Deposition of Captain John Parker Concerning the Battle at Lexington*, April 25, 1775, document No. 4, National Archives identifier 595246, Record Group 360. [DocsTeach transcript, image, citation, and passages](SOURCES.md#the-specific-militia-deposition). | `depositions`; Parker's name and date; his no-fire order and claim of an unprovoked attack by regulars | Specific transcript and metadata checked on DocsTeach. Label the card as Parker's April 25 deposition summary. Do not imply an unidentified collective account or a settled first-shot verdict. |\n| `[DOOLITTLE]` | Amos Doolittle, *The Battle of Lexington, April 19, 1775. Plate I*. Yale University Art Gallery [object 2482](https://artgallery.yale.edu/collections/objects/2482), accession 1920.25a-d. Image and rights citation above. | `doolittle`; image title, Yale accession, CC0 label, captions, and scoring explanations | Shows how an artist pictured the British firing. The image is not proof of the first shooter. Local images are present; collection metadata and rights are inherited, not re-fetched. |\n| `[SETTING]` | NPS, *April 19, 1775*, 5:00 a.m. entry; [setting and numbers references](SOURCES.md#lexington-setting-and-numbers). `[DOOLITTLE]` also gives the date in its title. | Lexington, Massachusetts, dawn on April 19, 1775; regulars and militia on the Green | Public historical passage checked. Camera, model placement, and simplified map remain reconstructions. |\n| `[NIGHT]` | Lexington History Museums, *Paul Revere's Ride*, April 18th, 1775 section. [Checked warning and escape passages](SOURCES.md#night-alarm-and-escape). | Night alarm; Revere's warning and ride; Clarke parsonage; Adams and Hancock's escape | Accessible museum account replaces private BrainLift support. Later recollections and differing arrival times are noted. Eli's route, horse, errands, and exchanges remain dramatized. |\n| `[NUMBERS]` | Thomas Rice Willard, April 23, 1775 deposition, Tomlinson's 1855 supplement, pages 97 to 98; Robins, NPS timeline and expedition overview; Gould, Revere, and Barker estimates. [Checked passages and scope comparison](SOURCES.md#lexington-setting-and-numbers). | `numbers`; qualified opening caption; inherited approximate 400 regulars versus 80 militia reconstruction | Willard estimates about 400 regulars; Robins about 1,000. NPS gives about 200 for a detached advance force and 77 militia; cited militia estimates span about 50 to 300. Show scopes and uncertainty beside the reconstruction, not an exact headcount. Keep inherited scoring unchanged. |\n| `[FORMATION]` | *Battles of Lexington and Concord*, Lexington section, citing Galvin, pages 120 to 124; Parkhurst and thirteen other deponents, April 25, 1775, Tomlinson supplement, pages 103 to 104. [Checked passages](SOURCES.md#formation). | `formation`; parade formation in plain sight; opening and muster captions | Accessible secondary passage and supporting primary deposition checked. Galvin's book was not independently read. Exact model spacing is a reconstruction. |\n| `[COMMAND]` | *Battles of Lexington and Concord*, Lexington section, citing Fischer, pages 189 to 190, and militia depositions. [Checked movement and command passages](SOURCES.md#commands-and-named-person-movements). | Adair's turn onto the Common; Pitcairn's arrival from the rear; some men not hearing Parker's raspy command | Public secondary passages checked; underlying Fischer pages not independently read. Willard also describes not understanding an officer. Exact dialogue, performed volume, locations, and timing in gameplay are dramatized. |\n| `[OUTCOME]` | NPS timeline, 5:00 a.m. entry; *Battles of Lexington and Concord*, British advance to Concord section, citing Quarles, page 10. [Checked casualty passages](SOURCES.md#outcome). | `outcome`; 8 colonists killed, 10 wounded, and 1 British soldier wounded | Public accounts checked; figures concern Lexington alone. Quarles's book was not independently read. |\n| `[CONTROL]` | Barker's diary in NPS Reading 2; *Battles of Lexington and Concord*, British advance to Concord section, citing Fischer, pages 198 to 200. [Checked control passages](SOURCES.md#officers-control-and-barkers-account). | `control`; regulars exceeding officers' control | Barker describes a rush without orders; the secondary account describes loss and restoration of control. Fischer's book was not independently read. No proven officer motive or first-shot command is asserted. |\n| `[PIVOT]` | Compare `[R75]`, `[R98]`, `[BARKER]`, and `[MILITIA]`. | Unresolved-first-shot caption; reason the camera conceals the pivot | Editorial synthesis of conflicting accounts and their limits. Does not assert that no person could have seen a first shooter. |\n\nUse a source ID and an accessible citation for every displayed fact, caption, and source label, including cutscene text. Keep the full reference here, with a short source label in the notebook. Verify quotations against the selected transcription and preserve full sentences or mark omissions with ellipses. Brief-only support does not satisfy historical citation fidelity. References and relevant passages belong in the design packet before design acceptance, or the claim must be explicitly dramatized. DQ-08, DQ-11, DQ-12, and DQ-17 record the source and estimate defaults.\n\n## Seed content repairs for playable slices\n\nWL-D1 corrects this register and defines replacement content; it does not alter game code. Before reuse, fix Barker summaries in `data/evidence.js`, its essay-reference label in `data/essay-rules.js`, and any fixture essays to **one or two shots**. Apply the artist framing to every Doolittle summary and scoring explanation, then regenerate `SCORING.md`. Use MHS for Revere 1775 and MASSAR with `&amp;` and its editorial flag for 1798. Identify Parker's April 25 deposition on the militia card and show the numbers card's estimates and scope note. The seed retains text defects; the historical references required by this design review are now supplied here and in `SOURCES.md`.\n\n## Code &amp; scene\n\n### WL-S1 displayed script register\n\n`data/night-ride.js` contains the complete Slice A captions, dialogue, notebook observations, and source labels. `src/night-ride.js` adds task instructions, controls, save notices, and the completion screen. This script handoff is `kind: design` as well as a code handoff.\n\n- Opening date and place resolve to `[SETTING]`; parade formation to `[FORMATION]`; the qualified force caption to `[NUMBERS]`. The displayed NPS, deposition, and secondary-account notebook labels resolve to the matching source-register rows above and passages in `SOURCES.md`.\n- The opening subtitle \"Dawn of April 19 · reconstructed opening\" uses the date and setting in `[SETTING]`. It labels reconstructed staging, not an eyewitness view.\n- The blackout sentence \"These accounts do not settle who fired first\" resolves to `[PIVOT]`, comparing `[R75]`, `[R98]`, `[BARKER]`, and `[MILITIA]`. The opening calls no firing animation and reveals no shooter. It is an editorial synthesis, not primary testimony.\n- The rewind date, parsonage label, Revere warning context, and notebook account of Adams and Hancock resolve to `[NIGHT]` and [the checked museum passage](SOURCES.md#night-alarm-and-escape). No precise arrival time or invented escape scene is shown.\n- Revere's two lines are the WL-D1 proposed **dramatized dialogue**. The residents' \"That is what I was told\" line is the proposed **fictional character** dialogue. All speech carries its label on screen. No speech is presented as a primary-source quotation.\n- Eli, the borrowed horse, Lantern and Orchard households, their residents, the alarm errands, and personal notebook observations are invented gameplay. The lane and building geometry are simplified reconstructions, not historical routes or exact architecture. \"Parsonage · reconstruction\", \"Lantern · fictional\", and \"Orchard · fictional\" identify those limits in the scene.\n- The completion reflection distinguishes receiving a warning from personally seeing regulars. It is a lesson about Eli's fictional observations, not new testimony about the first shot. The slice has no fact cards or MHS item labels, and does not load the unrepaired v1 evidence or essay UI. Their text repair remains with WL-S3 before reuse.\n\n### WL-S2 displayed script register\n\n`data/dawn-muster.js` supplies Slice B's caption, dialogue, listening text, notebook records, and command source label. `src/night-ride.js` supplies its message relay, controls, transition, and checkpoint text. This handoff is `kind: design` as well as a code handoff.\n\n- The dawn date and Lexington Green caption resolve to `[SETTING]` and `[FORMATION]`, using the passages already registered in [SOURCES.md](SOURCES.md#formation). The militia models remain a simplified reconstruction, without an exact headcount.\n- Parker's \"Keep together. Listen for orders.\" is the approved WL-D1 dramatized line, identified on screen as invented words, not a quotation. His name, militia context, and the notebook's command label resolve to `[FORMATION]` and `[COMMAND]` and [the command passage](SOURCES.md#commands-and-named-person-movements).\n- The hearing context is a visibly labeled document paraphrase of `[COMMAND]`: the cited secondary account describes some men not hearing Parker's raspy command. The near text repeats the dramatized Parker line. The distant text deliberately omits words in a simulated hearing task, explicitly identified as neither a historical quotation nor a transcription of an actual listener's experience. This silent text experiment supplies no historical measurement of distance, volume, or intent.\n- The fictional militiaman and townsperson use the proposed WL-D1 lines. Their question buttons, Eli's message relay, notebook observations, stop locations, and spectator boundary are invented. All carry fictional or dramatized labels. The boundary is a game safety marker, not a documented historical line.\n- The near/far reflection concerns only Eli's simulated hearing. No named figure gives an invented first-shot account, and no shot or combat occurs in Slice B. The unrepaired v1 evidence and essay scripts remain unreachable; WL-S3 owns those repairs before reuse.\n\n- Lexington Green low-poly scene and globe transition adapted from `workbench-forge` Witness History v2.1 (`buildLexington` + geographic transition).\n- three.js r186 + OrbitControls, bundled locally (no CDN).\n- Coastline data: Natural Earth 1:50m land (public domain).\n\n### WL-S3 displayed script register\n\nThis is a `kind: design` content handoff as well as a code handoff. `data/green.js` supplies the Green captions and fictional observations. `src/green-essay.js` supplies the modern study, hypothesis, and essay instructions. `data/evidence.js` supplies every card's source ID, source label, passage anchor, and limitation.\n\n- The advance-company turn and Pitcairn arrival caption resolve to `[COMMAND]`. Their animation timing, Eli's two spectator stops, staged obstruction, and shelter are visibly reconstructed or fictional. No named person receives new dialogue. The report and subsequent firing are silent text simulations. No shooter plan or first-shot effect runs.\n- Pivot uncertainty and completion captions resolve to `[PIVOT]`. The retreat and neutral shelter casualty statement resolve to `[OUTCOME]` and `[CONTROL]`. The camera conceals the origin at both spectator spots. Fictional scene notes cannot count as primary testimony.\n- The nine historical cards and all source labels resolve to their existing rows in this register. Revere 1775 uses a marked MHS trim and a separate labeled paraphrase for smoke and the obstructed view. Revere 1798 uses the marked MASSAR excerpt with `&amp;` and its struck-through paragraph flag; MHS item 99 labels manuscript provenance only.\n- Barker's summaries and essay-reference label say **one or two shots**. NPS's published diary excerpt is the chosen source. Parker's card identifies his April 25 deposition, document No. 4, NARA 595246, and uses a summary. Every Doolittle scoring explanation describes how an artist pictured the British firing. The displayed Yale title, accession, image, and CC0 label use the inherited citation above.\n- The numbers card displays the estimate and scope note before scoring. Scoring marks, allowed locations, combination rule, and essay thresholds are inherited. Ranking is an unscored source-reflection task. A pattern-check pass does not settle the first shot or certify authorship.\n- WL-S3 made no network requests to recheck historical sources. Quotations and metadata use the checked source packet already in this checkout. Original document scans are honestly labeled unavailable; the Doolittle local image is present.\n\n### WL-S4 displayed content repairs\n\nThis handoff is `kind: design` for the essay instructions, contradiction feedback, and staged-view caption. The obstruction label describes fictional staging and retains `[PIVOT]`. No new historical dialogue or claims were added. The Revere 1798 excerpt retains the chosen MASSAR ampersands, its editorial flag, and the terminal semicolon followed by an ellipsis marking the trim. Formation scoring now says parade formation in plain sight under `[FORMATION]`, removing unsupported rank precision. Numbers explicitly says reconstruction estimate under `[NUMBERS]`. Contradiction explanations still use the same cited table and unchanged marks. Essay and authorship instructions describe local checks and teacher review, not historical evidence. Built artifacts contain this source register and SOURCES.md so citations remain available offline.\n</pre>"],{type:"text/html"}));
(function(){var s=document.createElement("style");s.textContent=".wl { position:relative; overflow:hidden; font:15px/1.4 system-ui,sans-serif; color:#f4f1e7; background:#101e36; }\n.wl * { box-sizing:border-box; }\n.wl [hidden] { display:none !important; }\n.wl button, .wl a { font:inherit; }\n.wl button { min-height:44px; padding:9px 14px; border:1px solid #8b9aaa; border-radius:8px; background:#243751; color:#fff; cursor:pointer; }\n.wl button:hover { background:#344c68; }\n.wl button:disabled { opacity:.55; cursor:default; }\n.wl button:focus-visible, .wl a:focus-visible { outline:3px solid #ffda8d; outline-offset:3px; }\n.wl button.primary { background:#ffda8d; border-color:#ffda8d; color:#172338; font-weight:700; }\n.wl a { color:#ffda8d; display:inline-flex; align-items:center; min-height:44px; min-width:44px; padding:8px 0; }\n.wl h1 { font-size:clamp(22px,4vw,32px); margin:0 0 8px; }\n.wl h2 { font-size:22px; margin:0 0 10px; }\n.wl p { margin:6px 0 12px; }\n.wl small { font-size:12px; color:#d0d9e5; }\n.wl .row { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:8px; align-items:center; }\n.wl .top { position:absolute; inset:10px 10px auto; z-index:2; display:flex; justify-content:space-between; gap:12px; align-items:start; pointer-events:none; }\n.wl .top > * { pointer-events:auto; }\n.wl .brand { padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:#142237ee; }\n.wl .brand b, .wl .brand small { display:block; }\n.wl .panel { position:absolute; z-index:2; bottom:12px; left:12px; right:12px; background:#142237f2; padding:12px 16px; border:1px solid #738392; border-radius:12px; max-height:48%; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px; overflow:hidden; }\n.wl .panel p { margin:0 0 6px; }\n.wl .overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:3; display:grid; place-items:center; background:#0b1427a8; padding:12px; }\n.wl .overlay.black { background:#000; }\n.wl .card { width:min(570px,100%); max-height:100%; overflow:auto; border:1px solid #738392; border-radius:12px; background:#142237; padding:22px; }\n.wl .black .card { background:#000; border:0; }\n.wl .tag { color:#ffda8d; font-size:12px; font-weight:700; margin:0 0 6px; }\n.wl .notice { padding:6px 10px; color:#ffda8d; background:#142237; font-size:12px; }\n.wl dialog { color:#f4f1e7; background:#142237; border:1px solid #738392; border-radius:12px; width:min(560px,calc(100% - 24px)); max-height:calc(100% - 32px); padding:18px; }\n.wl dialog::backdrop { background:#080f1de0; }\n.wl dialog article { border-top:1px solid #536478; padding:8px 0; }\n.wl dialog header { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; gap:12px; align-items:center; }\n.wl dialog .close { position:sticky; bottom:-18px; background:#142237; padding:10px 0 0; }\n.wl .route button { font-size:13px; padding:8px 10px; }\n@media (max-height:450px) {\n  .wl { font-size:13px; }\n  .wl .card { padding:14px 18px; }\n  .wl .card h1 { font-size:24px; }\n  .wl .panel { padding:8px 12px; max-height:46%; }\n  .wl .brand { padding:5px 10px; }\n  .wl .top { inset:6px 8px auto; }\n  .wl button { padding:7px 10px; }\n}\n\n.wl .card label { display:block; margin:12px 0 4px; font-weight:600; }\n.wl .card select, .wl .card textarea { display:block; width:100%; min-height:44px; background:#0c182c; color:#f4f1e7; border:1px solid #8b9aaa; border-radius:6px; padding:10px; font:inherit; }\n.wl .card textarea { resize:vertical; }\n.wl .card select:focus-visible, .wl .card textarea:focus-visible { outline:3px solid #ffda8d; outline-offset:2px; }\n.wl .document-image { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; margin:12px 0; }\n.wl blockquote { margin:12px 0; border-left:3px solid #ffda8d; padding:8px 12px; }\n.wl #hypothesis-result, .wl #essay-result { margin:12px 0; }\n.wl .card > button { margin:6px 8px 2px 0; }\n\n/* Primary scene actions stay above the scrolling travel choices. */\n.wl .panel > p { flex:none; }\n.wl .panel > .row:not(.route) { order:1; flex:none; }\n.wl .panel > .route { order:2; min-height:0; overflow:auto; flex-shrink:1; }\n.wl .panel > .notice { order:3; flex:none; margin:0; }\n/* Study content scrolls independently of the action footer. */\n.wl .card.study-card { display:flex; flex-direction:column; overflow:hidden; padding:0; }\n.wl .card-scroll { min-height:0; overflow:auto; padding:18px 22px; }\n.wl .card-actions { flex:none; display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; padding:10px 14px; border-top:1px solid #738392; background:#142237; }\n.wl .card-actions button { flex:1 1 44%; }\n@media (max-height:450px) {\n  .wl .card.study-card { padding:0; }\n  .wl .card-scroll { padding:12px 18px; }\n  .wl .card-actions { padding:6px 10px; }\n  .wl .panel > p { max-height:3.8em; overflow:auto; }\n}\n";document.head.appendChild(s);})();
